# EOL normalization rules for cross-platform checkouts.
#
# F5-13 audit fix: the previous absence of this file meant Windows
# checkouts of ``install.ps1`` / ``start_app.vbs`` / ``stop_app.bat``
# could normalize to CRLF (Git's ``core.autocrlf=true`` default) and
# silently break the very scripts Windows users need to bootstrap.
# The rules below match what those scripts require:
#
#   * Windows shell scripts (PowerShell, VBScript, cmd) and Dockerfiles
#     MUST keep CRLF — they are line-ending-sensitive at parse time
#     (PowerShell `r` is a statement terminator, VBScript is similar).
#   * All other text files default to LF — the canonical form for
#     Python, YAML, Markdown, JavaScript, etc.
#   * Binary files are marked ``-text`` so Git's diff machinery doesn't
#     try to mangle them on checkout.
#
# The ``_check_eol.ps1`` helper script can be run after a checkout to
# confirm the rules actually stuck (the script itself uses CRLF and
# would have been mangled without this file).

# Windows shell scripts — must keep CRLF on checkout.
*.ps1    text eol=crlf
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.vbs    text eol=crlf

# Dockerfiles — variable substitution on Windows can fail on LF-only
# lines that Git rewrote to CRLF mid-script; pin to LF.
Dockerfile        text eol=lf
.dockerignore     text eol=lf

# Default for everything else: LF.
*        text eol=lf

# Binary assets.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.tar     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tgz     binary
*.whl     binary
*.so      binary
*.dll     binary
*.pyd     binary
